# act (local GitHub Actions runner) configuration — repo-root `.actrc`.
#
# act reads this file automatically from the working directory, and it takes
# precedence over a machine-local `~/.config/act/actrc`, so a plain
#
#     act -W .github/workflows/ci.yml
#
# reproduces hosted CI here without extra flags.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# act's own default runner image is the bare Debian `node:22-bookworm`, which
# ships no Python/pip, no `make`, no docker CLI and no populated tool cache.
# GitHub's `ubuntu-latest` is Ubuntu 24.04, and every job in these workflows is
# written for that platform, so on a Debian node image the jobs fail for
# runner-image reasons that do not exist in hosted CI (`pip: command not
# found`, missing build tools) — a "local CI is red" signal that says nothing
# about the workflows. `catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest` is the GitHub runner
# image itself (it reports ImageOS=ubuntu24, the same base as `ubuntu-latest`)
# and carries the Python/pip, make, file and docker CLI these jobs use.
# Verified against the real thing: the hosted runs of every job in this
# workflow are green at the same commit (gh run view <id> --json jobs).
#
# The tag is the rolling "current GitHub runner image" tag on purpose: the
# point of this pin is fidelity to `ubuntu-latest`, not a frozen distro.
-P ubuntu-latest=catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest
# Keep the container platform explicit so runs are identical on arm64 hosts.
--container-architecture linux/amd64
#
# Run one job at a time. act mounts a SINGLE shared `act-toolcache` volume at
# /opt/hostedtoolcache for every job in a run, and `actions/setup-go` installs
# the Go SDK into it. On a cold cache, parallel jobs race on the same
# directory tree and corrupt it, and every matrix leg goes red for a reason
# that cannot happen on GitHub Actions (each hosted job gets its own fresh
# VM with a pre-populated tool cache):
#
#   File was unable to be removed Error: ENOTEMPTY: directory not empty,
#   rmdir '/opt/hostedtoolcache/go/1.26.9/x64'   -> exitcode '1': failure
#
# Reproduced on purpose (2026-10-09) by clearing the tool cache and running
# `act -j cross-build`: all 5 matrix legs failed; the same command against a
# warm cache is green.
#
# It is also what makes the COLD case safe: act starts the legs of a matrix job
# as one batch (they ignore this limit), but the first non-matrix Go job
# (Build & Test) now runs ALONE and warms the cache first, so every leg after it
# reports "Found in cache" instead of downloading into the same directory at the
# same time. Verified by clearing the tool cache again and running the whole
# workflow with this file: exit 0, 10/10 jobs green.
#
# Once the cache is warm, parallelism can be restored from the command line
# (`act --concurrent-jobs <n> ...` — CLI flags win over this file).
--concurrent-jobs 1
#
# A machine-local service on a port a job binds must be stopped for the run.
# The Postgres Integration job publishes 5432 on the host (`docker run -p
# 5432:5432`) and act runs job containers with host networking, so a native
# PostgreSQL on 5432 makes that job fail with
#
#   failed to bind host port 0.0.0.0:5432/tcp: address already in use
#
# i.e. a host-state red, not a workflow defect (hosted runners are clean).
# Stop it before the run and start it again afterwards, or run on a box with
# no local PostgreSQL.
